<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<bs:grid
        xmlns:bs="https://causeway.apache.org/applib/layout/grid/bootstrap3"
        xmlns:cpt="https://causeway.apache.org/applib/layout/component">

    <!-- Top Header & Unassigned Actions.
         Causeway requires a column (or tabGroup) carrying unreferencedCollections="true"
         in EVERY layout XML, even when the type has no collections — without it the
         whole grid is rejected with a metadataError and silently replaced by the default. -->
    <bs:row>
        <bs:col span="12" unreferencedActions="true" unreferencedCollections="true">
            <cpt:domainObject/>
        </bs:col>
    </bs:row>

    <!-- ONE VIEW, ONE PANEL — AND WHY IT IS STILL A TAB.

         WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: without a layout XML Causeway falls back to its bundled
         GridFallbackLayout.xml, which puts every property in a span-4 column — so the diagram got
         ~1/3 of the page and was unreadable.  Nothing else can widen it: the extension's own
         D3Panel.css already sets `width: 100%`, so the enclosing grid column is the ONLY width
         driver — same reason, same shape as UmlDiagram.layout.xml and OntologyDiagram.layout.xml.

         WHY ONE TAB AND NOT A STACK: the page used to offer three views of one artifact — the
         Kroki/PDF rendering, the D3 picture, and the PlantUML source — and stacked they were three
         screens tall, so a reader who did not scroll reported the page as having one diagram
         (content below the horizon is content a reader does not know exists).  The PDF view and the
         source view are gone: the D3 picture *is* the diagram, and the PlantUML text is a download
         on DomainModels — a page is for the picture, a file is for the source.  The tab group is
         kept rather than collapsed to a plain fieldSet, because the panel is still one artifact of
         several possible ones and the tab is what the browser remembers.

         THE SUB-DOMAIN IS NOT IN THE TAB LABEL, and cannot be: a Causeway tab name comes from this
         file, one layout serves every instance of the view model, and `BSTab` carries nothing but a
         name — so the label cannot vary per diagram (verified against causeway-applib: `BSTab`
         exposes `getName`/`setName` only, and `TabGroupPanel.tabsFor` labels each tab from it).
         It is carried by the property's own label instead — `UmlClassDiagram#namedD3Graph()`, a
         member-support method, which is re-read per instance and reads "Sub-domain: Inventory" /
         "Whole model" / "Sub-domain overview".

         The column spans ALL 12 columns, because width is what makes the panel readable.  A tab
         panel is built when its tab is shown (AjaxBootstrapTabbedPanel puts only the selected tab's
         panel in the DOM — verified live), so the panel never measures a hidden container; the D3
         picture takes its viewBox from the payload's own canvas, not from the container.

         The memento properties (domainModel, kind, subDomainName) are hidden (Where.EVERYWHERE) and
         already shown in the object title; unreferencedProperties must still be set somewhere, or
         the whole grid is rejected. -->
    <bs:row>
        <bs:col span="12">
            <bs:tabGroup>
                <bs:tab name="Diagram">
                    <bs:row>
                        <bs:col span="12">
                            <cpt:fieldSet name="Diagram" id="content" unreferencedProperties="true">
                                <cpt:property id="d3Graph"/>
                            </cpt:fieldSet>
                        </bs:col>
                    </bs:row>
                </bs:tab>
            </bs:tabGroup>
        </bs:col>
    </bs:row>
</bs:grid>
